WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.776
Output validation: check what came back against facts you control.

2
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:09.240
Output guards work best when they compare the model’s output with something the model can’t change.

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:10.373
Schemas.

4
00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:15.283
Structured outputs guarantee shape, not truth (you learned this in Studio 1).

5
00:00:15.563 --> 00:00:20.491
Keep the schema check, and keep the cross-field rules and evidence checks that sit on top of it.

6
00:00:20.771 --> 00:00:21.688
Groundedness.

7
00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:32.272
For anything that answers from documents, verify citations in code: the cited passage was actually retrieved, and the quoted text appears in it (Studio 2).

8
00:00:32.552 --> 00:00:38.504
Where the claim needs judgment, a narrow LLM check (“does this passage support this sentence?

9
00:00:38.784 --> 00:00:41.664
”) helps, measured against your labels like any judge.

10
00:00:41.944 --> 00:00:43.843
PII the user didn’t supply.

11
00:00:44.123 --> 00:00:55.195
If the reply contains an email or phone number that wasn’t in the user’s own message, it probably came from somewhere it shouldn’t, such as another customer’s record retrieved by a tool.

12
00:00:55.475 --> 00:00:56.819
Redact it and log it.

13
00:00:57.099 --> 00:00:58.549
System prompt leakage.

14
00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:04.013
Plant a random canary string in the system prompt and block any output containing it.

15
00:01:04.293 --> 00:01:19.355
Anthropic’s guidance on prompt leaks recommends exactly this kind of post-processing, and warns that elaborate leak-proofing instructions can degrade task performance; it also says to leave out proprietary details the model doesn’t need.

16
00:01:19.635 --> 00:01:21.960
A canary detects verbatim leaks only.

17
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:29.664
A model can paraphrase its instructions, which is why the real fix is having nothing secret in the prompt (lesson 11).

18
00:01:29.944 --> 00:01:31.181
Links and actions.

19
00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:36.411
Allow links only to hosts you list, and compare hostnames, not substrings ( help.

20
00:01:36.691 --> 00:01:37.544
example.

21
00:01:37.824 --> 00:01:38.549
com.

22
00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:39.555
evil.

23
00:01:39.835 --> 00:01:41.456
test contains your domain).

24
00:01:41.736 --> 00:01:48.221
If output will be rendered as HTML, escape it: lesson 11’s exercise builds that sanitizer.
